{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Outlier.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Outlier.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"outlier outlier","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Outlier.md","fileName":"Outlier.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Outlier","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-13 10:25\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Outlier Detection and DBSCAN]] · [[Exploratory and Robust Data Analysis]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Outlier Detection and DBSCAN","Exploratory and Robust Data Analysis","clusteranalysisanddatamining.pdf","dataanalysisforthelifescienceswithr.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Outlier.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Outlier.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"outlier outlier","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Outlier.md","fileName":"Outlier.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Outlier","heading":"Outlier","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"An outlier is an observation that deviates enough from other observations to suggest it may have arisen from a different mechanism. It can be an error, a rare valid case, or evidence of a distinct process.\n\nDeclaring a point anomalous requires an explicit reference context. Global distance, local density, statistical probability, and cluster membership can produce different judgments.\n\nIn life-science measurements, an outlier can arise from an undesired technical process such as scanner damage or amplification bias. The source demonstrates that one extreme value can severely distort the sample mean, variance, correlation, and t-test, motivating robust and rank-based alternatives.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Outlier Detection and DBSCAN","Exploratory and Robust Data Analysis","clusteranalysisanddatamining.pdf","dataanalysisforthelifescienceswithr.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageE